WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:05.520
Horror is all about surprise. And these moments&nbsp;
-- including an accidental electrocution,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:10.960
a cannibal's connection to a bloodsucker, and&nbsp;
a creepy inside joke -- surprised everyone.

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:17.200
At the conclusion of the classic 1996 slasher&nbsp;
Scream, we finally learn who's behind the mask&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:22.320
of the ghost-faced killer that's been terrorizing&nbsp;
the local teens of Woodsboro. It turns out there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:28.000
wasn't just one knife-wielding maniac, but two:&nbsp;
Billy, the bad boy boyfriend of heroine Sidney,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:34.480
and agent of chaos Stu. Unlike Billy, Stu doesn't&nbsp;
have a personal vendetta or a tragic dark past.

00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:38.960
So when this deadly duo enact the final step&nbsp;
of their plan by stabbing each other to make it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:44.320
appear as though Sidney's dad attacked them, Stu&nbsp;
doesn't exactly take it very well. He babbles and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:48.480
hurls obscenities with the shock of a child&nbsp;
who has just learned what consequences are.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:53.680
Amidst the chaos, Sidney slips away, calls the&nbsp;
cops, and proceeds to antagonize her assailants&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.680 --> 00:00:58.640
over the phone. A frustrated Billy then throws the&nbsp;
phone across the room and it hits Stu in the head.

00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:02.320
"Ow. You f---ing hit me with the phone, d---!"

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:08.000
Scream writer Kevin Williamson revealed on the&nbsp;
movie's Blu-ray commentary that Matthew Lillard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:13.120
improvised a huge chunk of his lines, including&nbsp;
the one in response to being hit with the phone.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:17.760
In fact, this whole moment was unplanned, as&nbsp;
Skeet Ulrich was supposed to throw the phone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:21.760
at the kitchen counter, but it slipped out&nbsp;
of his hands that were covered in fake blood.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:26.800
So when he accidentally hit his co-star&nbsp;
instead, Lillard promptly retorted in character.

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:31.840
When aspiring FBI agent Clarice&nbsp;
Starling pays a visit to imprisoned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:35.200
serial killer Hannibal Lecter,&nbsp;
she learns the truth about him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:40.240
via a vivid anecdote from the man himself&nbsp;
about his encounter with a census taker.

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:46.346
"I ate his liver with some&nbsp;
fava beans and a nice Chianti."

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:55.200
Anthony Hopkins' delivery as Lecter is inhumane&nbsp;
and terrifyingly visceral. It's a perfectly creepy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.200 --> 00:02:00.480
thing for a madman like Hannibal and a disturbing&nbsp;
demonstration of vulgarity. The slurping noise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:04.880
that he makes wasn't actually in The Silence of&nbsp;
the Lambs' screenplay but was instead something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:10.400
the actor added in at the last minute. In a&nbsp;
2016 interview with Radio Times, he revealed,

00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:13.440
"I don't know why but I've always&nbsp;
known what scares people. When I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:17.440
was a kid I'd tell the girls around&nbsp;
the street the story about Dracula."

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:22.160
He then reproduced Lecter's legendary hiss&nbsp;
and added that "They'd run screaming away."

00:02:22.880 --> 00:02:28.400
Jack Nicholson's performance as a writer losing&nbsp;
his mind in 1980's The Shining is filled with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:33.280
plenty of unforgettable moments. There are the&nbsp;
quiet opening scenes when his strained smile&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.280 --> 00:02:38.720
belies his simmering insanity. Then there are the&nbsp;
sequences when the Overlook Hotel begins to seduce&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:44.080
an increasingly boozed-up Jack Torrance, thereby&nbsp;
alienating him from reality itself. And then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:48.800
there's of course the film's final act, when he&nbsp;
tears through anyone foolish enough to wind up on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:54.400
the pointy end of his ax. And the latter features&nbsp;
one particularly memorable improvised moment.

00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:59.120
While Jack's wife Wendy cowers in the bathroom,&nbsp;
he hacks away at the feeble door with reckless&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:04.960
abandon. After creating a suitable crack, he peers&nbsp;
through the porthole and maniacally proclaims:

00:03:05.840 --> 00:03:07.320
"Here's Johnny!"

00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:08.320
"Ahh!"

00:03:08.320 --> 00:03:12.400
That line doesn't appear in either Stephen&nbsp;
King's novel or the film's screenplay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:17.360
as it was instead ad-libbed by Nicholson.&nbsp;
Maybe director Stanley Kubrick's infamously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.360 --> 00:03:21.920
exhaustive takes drove Nicholson over the&nbsp;
deep end, or maybe he's just a really big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:26.000
fan of late night host Johnny Carson.&nbsp;
Either way, it's a terrifying moment.

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:30.080
"Herrrrrrre's Johnny!"

00:03:30.080 --> 00:03:33.120
Just imagine you're out on a shark&nbsp;
hunt, and when you least expect it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:37.600
your prey emerges from the ocean's depths,&nbsp;
summoned by all that delicious chum you've been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.600 --> 00:03:43.280
dumping into the water. How would you respond?&nbsp;
Before this moment happens in 1975's Jaws,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.280 --> 00:03:48.400
police chief Martin Brody has seen the shark&nbsp;
before but only in mere glimpses. But now with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:52.880
the beast just feet from his face, he has a real&nbsp;
sense of what he and his men are up against.

00:03:53.840 --> 00:03:55.120
"You're gonna need a bigger boat."

00:03:56.000 --> 00:04:01.600
As Jaws screenwriter Carl Gottlieb recalled to&nbsp;
The Hollywood Reporter in 2016, Brody's famous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.600 --> 00:04:06.960
utterance wasn't a scripted line so much as it&nbsp;
was an in-joke among the filmmakers. The movie's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.960 --> 00:04:11.440
shoot suffered from numerous difficulties,&nbsp;
like producers refusing to spend money on a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.440 --> 00:04:16.800
bigger support boat to steady the barge carrying&nbsp;
all of the equipment. As Gottliebe explained,

00:04:16.800 --> 00:04:20.960
"It became a catchphrase for any time&nbsp;
anything went wrong — if lunch was late&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.960 --> 00:04:25.440
or the swells were rocking the camera, someone&nbsp;
would say, 'You're gonna need a bigger boat.'"

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:29.200
So Roy Scheider ad-libbed the line at&nbsp;
various points throughout the shoot to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.200 --> 00:04:34.000
try to get this inside gag into the final&nbsp;
cut. His persistence ultimately paid off in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:38.960
the scene when Brody gets up close and personal&nbsp;
with the great white. As Gottlieb remembered,

00:04:38.960 --> 00:04:42.560
"It was so appropriate and so real&nbsp;
and it came at the right moment."

00:04:43.360 --> 00:04:49.040
The chestburster scene in the 1979 sci-fi&nbsp;
horror classic Alien is an absolute nightmare.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.040 --> 00:04:54.240
While the crew of the Nostromo enjoys a pleasant&nbsp;
crew breakfast, it's clear that something terrible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:59.280
is about to happen. Sure, executive officer&nbsp;
Kane says he feels great. But he recently had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.280 --> 00:05:05.280
an alien horseshoe crab glued to his face. So it's&nbsp;
hardly surprising that the incubating baby alien&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.280 --> 00:05:10.640
cracks its way through his fragile human ribs and&nbsp;
rips its way through his guts. Then it flashes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.640 --> 00:05:15.040
its pearly whites before zipping off into the&nbsp;
air ducts to fully mature into the monstrous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.040 --> 00:05:19.680
killer that will make the crew members' lives a&nbsp;
living hell. But what really sells this scene are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.680 --> 00:05:24.240
the crew's reactions. They look genuinely&nbsp;
horrified. And that's because they were.

00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:28.960
John Hurt was the only actor who knew what was&nbsp;
going to happen, mainly because he was outfitted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.960 --> 00:05:33.840
with a prosthetic chest full of real animal&nbsp;
organs and a compressed blood machine. But the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.840 --> 00:05:38.480
rest of the cast was ushered off-set while Hurt&nbsp;
was strapped in so that they could be kept totally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.480 --> 00:05:43.520
in the dark. When they leaned in close over Hurt's&nbsp;
fake torso, they got an unexpected face full of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.520 --> 00:05:48.080
explosive viscera. Sometimes to make horror&nbsp;
movie history, you have to break a few ribs.

00:05:48.960 --> 00:05:52.160
Most of the entries on this&nbsp;
list feature human ingenuity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.160 --> 00:05:56.720
but this one is more about human error. As&nbsp;
revealed in the making-of documentary Never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.720 --> 00:06:02.080
Sleep Again: The Elm Street Legacy, the scene&nbsp;
in 1984's A Nightmare on Elm Street in which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.080 --> 00:06:06.720
a snoozing Glen Lantz is sucked through&nbsp;
his bed didn't exactly go as planned.

00:06:06.720 --> 00:06:11.280
If you've seen it, you surely remember the geyser&nbsp;
of blood that emerges from the bed after Glen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.280 --> 00:06:16.080
gets sucked in. And then gravity basically stops&nbsp;
working as the blood begins to slosh from wall&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.080 --> 00:06:21.280
to wall like a crimson tide, perfectly capturing&nbsp;
the nightmare logic at play with Freddy Krueger.

00:06:21.280 --> 00:06:26.240
But filming the scene was a nightmare unto itself.&nbsp;
It was shot in a rotating room that was flipped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.240 --> 00:06:31.440
upside down. The mission was simple: pour fake&nbsp;
blood into a hole and film it. The first problem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.440 --> 00:06:36.400
was that the red liquid was poured onto a lamp,&nbsp;
which electrocuted a crew member. On top of that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.400 --> 00:06:41.600
the now-electrified blood caused the room to roll&nbsp;
all the way over. Fortunately, though, nobody was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.600 --> 00:06:46.240
seriously injured, and the filmmakers were left&nbsp;
with a trippier sequence than they'd expected.

00:06:46.880 --> 00:06:49.360
"No one was hurt. We got the shot."

00:06:49.360 --> 00:06:56.000
Possessed preteen Regan MacNeil goes through hell&nbsp;
over the course of the 1973 classic The Exorcist.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.000 --> 00:07:01.120
And the most hellish moment is probably when&nbsp;
she projectile-vomits split pea soup onto Father&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.120 --> 00:07:06.960
Karras. Naturally enough, he reacts by gasping&nbsp;
for breath and looking appropriately disgusted.

00:07:06.960 --> 00:07:10.800
But if you look really closely, you can&nbsp;
also detect a hint of uncharacteristic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.800 --> 00:07:15.120
anger on the usually even-tempered&nbsp;
priest. This is because Jason Miller,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.120 --> 00:07:19.280
the actor who played him, wasn't clued&nbsp;
in to what exactly was going to happen.

00:07:19.280 --> 00:07:24.480
In the BBC documentary The Fear of God: 25&nbsp;
Years of 'The Exorcist', the movie's head of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.480 --> 00:07:28.800
special effects revealed that he was all set&nbsp;
up to have the vomit hit Miller in the chest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.800 --> 00:07:34.000
as scripted and rehearsed. But delays in setting&nbsp;
up the scene led to the vomit getting cold,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.000 --> 00:07:38.640
so the trajectory of the rig was changed&nbsp;
and tilted it up towards Miller's face,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.640 --> 00:07:41.840
and his unscripted reaction&nbsp;
made it into the final cut.

00:07:42.480 --> 00:07:46.640
1996's From Dusk Till Dawn is what&nbsp;
you get when you throw vampires,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.640 --> 00:07:51.760
Westerns, and siege thrillers all together in&nbsp;
a blender. One scene finds two bad brothers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.760 --> 00:07:57.520
played by George Clooney and Quentin Tarantino, in&nbsp;
a lavish watering hole called the Titty Twister,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.520 --> 00:08:00.000
awaiting the arrival of a partner in crime.

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:04.320
Soon after the duo arrive, a stripper&nbsp;
by the name of Santanico Pandemonium&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.320 --> 00:08:09.280
performs a snake dance striptease routine. This&nbsp;
is before the brothers discover that this bar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.280 --> 00:08:14.320
in the middle of nowhere is also an ancient&nbsp;
Aztec haunt for a coven of blood suckers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.320 --> 00:08:18.640
So at least the dance is an enjoyable&nbsp;
perk to their supposedly calm rendezvous.

00:08:18.640 --> 00:08:24.560
Santanico was an early role for Salma Hayek.&nbsp;
In a 2017 interview with Yahoo, she admitted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.560 --> 00:08:28.960
that she has very few memories of filming&nbsp;
the striptease scene because, as she put it,

00:08:28.960 --> 00:08:31.200
"I had to go on trance to do the dance."

00:08:31.200 --> 00:08:36.000
This was all a matter of the actress overcoming&nbsp;
her fear of snakes. As she further explained,

00:08:36.000 --> 00:08:38.720
"There was no choreography. It was improvised.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.720 --> 00:08:42.080
Because you can’t choreograph a snake,&nbsp;
we don’t know what she’s going to do!"

00:08:42.800 --> 00:08:48.640
"Kneel and worship at the feet&nbsp;
of Santanico Pandemonium!"

00:08:48.640 --> 00:08:53.040
The Blair Witch Project wasn't the first found&nbsp;
footage horror film, but it's almost surely the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.040 --> 00:08:58.480
most influential. Filmed on commercially available&nbsp;
camera equipment, a big part of what makes Blair&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.480 --> 00:09:03.440
Witch so scary is its commitment to realism. It&nbsp;
really feels like we've stumbled across tapes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.440 --> 00:09:08.960
recording an ill-fated documentary crew. And a&nbsp;
big part of how directors Eduardo Sanchez and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.960 --> 00:09:14.320
Daniel Myrick captured that realistic vibe was&nbsp;
by having almost the entire film be improvised.

00:09:14.320 --> 00:09:19.120
As relayed in an oral history for Vice&nbsp;
celebrating Blair Witch's 20th anniversary,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.120 --> 00:09:24.480
the film's cast of three inexperienced, nonunion&nbsp;
actors were set loose in the Maryland wilderness&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.480 --> 00:09:29.680
to basically fend for themselves. They only had&nbsp;
one hazy sense of anything resembling a plan:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.680 --> 00:09:34.080
every night at the campsites, each actor&nbsp;
would be given a film canister in a milk crate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.080 --> 00:09:37.920
with top secret instructions that they&nbsp;
weren't allowed to share with each other.

00:09:37.920 --> 00:09:41.600
For instance, when Joshua Leonard mysteriously&nbsp;
vanished in the middle of the night,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.600 --> 00:09:45.440
his castmates had no idea that was&nbsp;
going to happen. As the actors tell it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.440 --> 00:09:49.120
they would've been genuinely terrified&nbsp;
if they weren't so damp, cold,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.120 --> 00:09:52.000
and exhausted from actually&nbsp;
camping while making a film.

00:09:52.880 --> 00:09:59.120
A 1978 remake of a 50s B-movie, the Philip&nbsp;
Kaufman-directed Invasion of the Body Snatchers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.120 --> 00:10:03.200
spins a terrifying yarn about spores&nbsp;
from outer space that quietly spread&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.200 --> 00:10:07.920
carbon copies of human beings to take over the&nbsp;
world. In the film's final moments, it appears&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.920 --> 00:10:12.720
that Donald Sutherland's Matthew has somehow&nbsp;
managed to evade the swarm of alien clones,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.720 --> 00:10:15.280
which is weird, because it&nbsp;
seemed like he'd been cornered.

00:10:15.280 --> 00:10:20.080
But now here he is, going peacefully about his&nbsp;
day. Maybe he's found a way to fly under the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.080 --> 00:10:25.040
radar and trick the aliens. That's what Veronica&nbsp;
Cartwright's Nancy hopes anyway when she sees&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.040 --> 00:10:30.320
her old friend. But then Matthew's jaw spills&nbsp;
open, with his eyes peeled wide, as he screams&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.320 --> 00:10:35.680
with the signature accusatory shriek of an alien&nbsp;
clone. Nancy is aghast, as are the rest of us.

00:10:40.517 --> 00:10:42.965
[screaming]

00:10:43.520 --> 00:10:48.960
And so was Cartwright, as it turns out. As Kaufman&nbsp;
explained to The Hollywood Reporter in 2018,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.960 --> 00:10:52.800
while Sutherland was clued into this&nbsp;
dire vision for the film's ending,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.800 --> 00:10:56.000
Cartwright was left in the&nbsp;
dark. As the director explained,

00:10:56.000 --> 00:11:00.480
"I don't remember that we even told Veronica&nbsp;
until Donald turned and did that shriek."

00:11:00.480 --> 00:11:05.360
Kaufman's desire was to make an ending that would&nbsp;
be truly horrific, which is why he didn't share it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.360 --> 00:11:11.440
with anyone, not even the studio executives. The&nbsp;
result was ultimately quite uncanny, as Cartwright&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.440 --> 00:11:16.080
looks genuinely shocked and terrified. That's&nbsp;
perfectly understandable, though, because if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.080 --> 00:11:20.842
you were suddenly faced with a shrieking Donald&nbsp;
Sutherland, you might be pretty terrified too.

